neutron: winewayland: take a key's VK from the active xkb layout, not from the sending thread's layout (206 regression)

Neutron#55 (@monkeysk4nk, US Colemak-DH, KDE Plasma 6.8.2 Wayland), on 11.10-206: "It now is acting as qwerty all the
time (ctrl or not) for me after the update, and photoshop is now behaving in qwerty too which it did not before. I tried
to relaunch neutron and the programs but no luck." On 187: typing followed Colemak-DH, only Ctrl shortcuts were QWERTY.

Mechanism: winewayland sends keys as KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE; win32u maps the scancode to a VK with the keyboard layout of
the SENDING thread (send_hardware_message: "TODO: Use the keyboard layout of the target hwnd"), while TranslateMessage
looks the character up in the app thread's layout. When the two tables differ, the VK comes from one table and the
character from the other. Before patch 206 every table used position VKs, so a mismatch only broke Ctrl (his 187
report). Patch 206 made the Colemak table map VK_D to 'd' (the D key), so a mismatch now also breaks text: the S key
gets VK_D from the other table and types 'd'. Patch 207 was meant to remove the mismatch by activating the active
layout on the event thread; on his desktop it evidently does not take effect (why: not determined, INFERRED). Lab
reproduction of his exact symptom: two xkb groups us,us(colemak_dh), Colemak group active, 206 with only
NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0: S key -> VK_D + 'd', Z key -> VK_B + 'b', Ctrl+S -> Ctrl+D (= "qwerty all the time");
with NEUTRON_LAYOUT_VKEYS=0 as well: 's'/'z' typed, Ctrl+D/Ctrl+B (= his 187).

Windows: the VK comes from the active keyboard layout's table ("The keyboard device driver receives scan codes ...
which are sent to the keyboard layout where they are translated", learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/inputdev/
about-keyboard-input, "Keyboard Input Model"), not from whichever thread happens to deliver the input.

Change: keyboard_handle_key() looks the scancode up in the ACTIVE xkb layout's own table (the layout whose HKL is
keyboard_hkl; base, E0 and E1 tables, with the KBD* flags) and sends wVk + wScan & 0xff (+ KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY), the
same request win32u builds from that table for KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE, as this file's lock-state and release paths already
do. No table entry -> the old scancode path. The VK no longer depends on the sending thread's layout; even if an app
thread keeps another table, letters stay right (any Latin table maps VK_S to 's'). Patch 207's activation stays.

Not covered: why 207's activation does not take effect on his desktop (single layout per his words; locale, KDE
keymap details and input methods not known); numpad keys not exercised (same table values incl. KBDNUMPAD, by code).

Lab (agent kb55, probe kbt.exe on nested KWin 6.8.2, runtime 11.10-206kb = 206 + this, x86_64 + i386 winewayland.so
built from 206's tree, pristine build byte-identical to 206's):
- us,us(colemak_dh), app started in the Colemak group: 206kb 2/2 's','z', VK_S/VK_Z, Ctrl+S/Ctrl+Z; 206kb with
  NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0 (207's activation off) 2/2 still right; 206kb with also NEUTRON_EVENT_VKEY=0 1/1 and 206
  with NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0 1/1: 'd','b', Ctrl+D/Ctrl+B (his symptom).
- fr under en_US with NEUTRON_EVENT_THREAD_HKL=0: AZERTY right (w, m) with this change.
- us, us(colemak_dh), fr: every WM_KEYDOWN/WM_CHAR/accelerator line identical between 206 and 206kb, including
  arrows, Home/End, Delete, PageUp (extended keys) and Alt+F; ru,us identical.
Kill switch: NEUTRON_EVENT_VKEY=0.
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
@@ -1195,6 +1195,54 @@
     NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd, 0, &input, 0);
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON event-vkey (Neutron#55, regression in 11.10-206): "It now is acting as qwerty all the time (ctrl or not)".
+ * We sent keys as KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE and win32u turned the scancode into a VK with the keyboard layout of the
+ * SENDING thread (send_hardware_message's TODO), while WM_CHAR is looked up in the app thread's layout. Whenever
+ * those two differ (a second xkb group, e.g. US + Colemak-DH, or a layout whose HKL is not the sending thread's),
+ * the VK came from another table: harmless while every table used position VKs, but since layout-letter-vkeys the
+ * Colemak table maps VK_D to 'd', so the S key (VK_D from the US table) typed 'd'. event-thread-hkl made the sending
+ * thread follow the active group, which covers the cases measured in the lab but evidently not every desktop.
+ * On Windows the VK comes from the active layout's table, whatever thread raw input runs on; so take it from the
+ * active xkb layout's own table here (with its KBD flags, exactly what win32u's scancode path would produce from
+ * that table) and send VK + scancode, as the lock-state and release paths in this file already do.
+ * NEUTRON_EVENT_VKEY=0 restores the scancode-only path. */
+static BOOL event_vkey_enabled(void)
+{
+    static int enabled = -1;
+    if (enabled < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_EVENT_VKEY");
+        enabled = !(e && e[0] == '0' && !e[1]);
+    }
+    return enabled;
+}
+
+/* The VK (with KBD* flags) the active xkb layout's table gives this scancode; 0 if none. Event thread only. */
+static WORD active_layout_vkey(UINT scan)
+{
+    struct layout *layout;
+    WORD vkey = 0;
+
+    if (!keyboard_hkl) return 0;
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&xkb_layouts_mutex);
+    LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(layout, &xkb_layouts, struct layout, entry)
+    {
+        const VSC_VK *entry;
+
+        if (keyboard_hkl != get_layout_hkl(layout, LOWORD(keyboard_hkl))) continue;
+        if (!(scan & 0x300)) vkey = layout->tables.pusVSCtoVK[scan & 0xff];
+        else
+        {
+            for (entry = (scan & 0x100) ? layout->tables.pVSCtoVK_E0 : layout->tables.pVSCtoVK_E1;
+                 entry && entry->Vsc; entry++)
+                if (entry->Vsc == (scan & 0xff)) { vkey = entry->Vk; break; }
+        }
+        break;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&xkb_layouts_mutex);
+    return (vkey & 0xff) == VK__none_ ? 0 : vkey;
+}
+
 static void keyboard_handle_key(void *data, struct wl_keyboard *wl_keyboard,
                                 uint32_t serial, uint32_t time, uint32_t key,
                                 uint32_t state)
@@ -1202,6 +1250,7 @@
     UINT scan = key2scan(key);
     INPUT input = {0};
     HWND hwnd;
+    WORD vkey;
 
     InterlockedExchange(&process_wayland.input_serial, serial);
 
@@ -1213,8 +1262,18 @@
     if (key == KEY_RIGHTALT) send_right_control(hwnd, state);
 
     input.type = INPUT_KEYBOARD;
-    input.ki.wScan = (scan & 0x300) ? scan + 0xdf00 : scan;
-    input.ki.dwFlags = KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE;
+    if (event_vkey_enabled() && (vkey = active_layout_vkey(scan)))
+    {
+        /* same request win32u builds for KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE: vkey from the table, scan & 0xff */
+        input.ki.wVk = vkey;
+        input.ki.wScan = scan & 0xff;
+        input.ki.dwFlags = 0;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        input.ki.wScan = (scan & 0x300) ? scan + 0xdf00 : scan;
+        input.ki.dwFlags = KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE;
+    }
     if (scan & ~0xff) input.ki.dwFlags |= KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY;
 
     if (state == WL_KEYBOARD_KEY_STATE_RELEASED) input.ki.dwFlags |= KEYEVENTF_KEYUP;
